**Proof.**

Yes, such a set exists. Consider the graph of the parabola restricted to the unit interval:

$$S = \{(x, x^2) : x \in [0,1]\} \subset [0,1]\times[0,1].$$

**$S$ is uncountable.** The map $x \mapsto (x, x^2)$ is a bijection from $[0,1]$ onto $S$. Since $[0,1]$ is uncountable, $S$ is uncountable.

**$S$ lies in the unit square.** For every $x \in [0,1]$, we have $x^2 \in [0,1]$, so $(x,x^2) \in [0,1]\times[0,1]$.

**Any line meets $S$ in at most finitely many points.** A line in the plane is given by an equation $ax + by + c = 0$ with $(a,b) \neq (0,0)$. Substituting a point $(x, x^2) \in S$ into the line equation gives

$$a x + b x^2 + c = 0,$$

i.e. $b x^2 + a x + c = 0$. This is a polynomial equation of degree at most $2$ in $x$, so it has at most $2$ real solutions. Hence the line contains at most $2$ points of $S$, which is certainly finite.

Thus $S$ is an uncountable subset of the unit square such that every line intersects it in at most $2$ (hence finitely many) points. $\blacksquare$

